{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>An Aramean sought to destroy my father.</b> Read it as though punctuated with a revocalization: my father Jacob was an Aramean — when he was in Aram he was אובד, meaning poor without property, for he held no possessions in the land." "<b>Destroying.</b> In the language of poverty, as in 'give strong drink לאובד, let him drink and forget his poverty.' And one should not wonder how Jacob is called an Aramean, for we find a parallel: Yitro the Ishmaelite — and he was an Israelite."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {0 "The lemma follows Rashi's reading of ארמי אבד אבי as a sentence (ארמי אובד אבי, 'an Aramean destroyed my father'), which Chizkuni then 'revocalizes' (סרסהו) to yield a different parsing; the note renders the hyper-literal sense of his instruction."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:54:20.742330927Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}